<p>Mandan's first municipal airport was Eielson Field, south of the eastern end of the area now commonly called the Strip. Although the airport was formally dedicated May 2, 1933, construction continued that July as workers cleared and stacked timber from the site. The City reserved the wood as home-heating fuel for the following winter in place of coal.</p>

<p>A February 6, 1934, letter from Governor {% person "william-wild-bill-langer" %}William Langer{% endperson %} to Washington attorney {% person "seth-w-richardson" %}Seth W. Richardson{% endperson %} provides a more detailed description of the airport. Langer placed Ben Eielson Field three miles west of the Bismarck post office and 3.1 miles east of the Mandan post office, along the concrete surface of U.S. Highway 10. He described a square, 160-acre tract with four runways ranging from 3,000 to 3,600 feet long. The field, he wrote, was entirely level, with sandy loam soil over a gravel subsoil, and could be maintained at reasonable expense.{% cite "site-description" %}</p>

<p>The letter involves an attempt to establish a joint airport for Bismarck and Mandan at Eielson Field. Mandan had proposed the arrangement, and Bismarck had rejected it. Langer's appeal in the letter urges approval and financial support for Mandan's proposal. He argued that Eielson Field's central location and capacity could serve both cities, while consolidating their airports would avoid duplicate equipment and maintenance costs.{% cite "joint-airport-proposal" %}</p>

<p>The joint airport effort ultimately did not succeed.</p>

